# Design: Left boundary on the token patterns

## Problem
Each token pattern matched its prefix inside other strings. A sql
result changed "task-add-device-revocation-regression-tests" to
"ta[redacted:openai-api-key]". On the live hub most hits of the
openai-api-key pattern sat inside identifiers and encoded data, so the
counts of ohya secrets scan were too high.

## Approach
The six token patterns now start with a boundary group: the start of
the text, or a character that is not a letter, a digit, a hyphen, or an
underscore. private-key-block and env-assignment are unchanged.

## Key Trade-offs
- Go regexp has no lookbehind. The boundary character stays in the
  output through the keep list of each pattern.
- A key that is glued to a letter, a digit, a hyphen, or an underscore
  is no longer redacted. A key in use is sent as one token after a
  separator, so the risk is accepted and stated in ADR 0015.
- An identifier that starts with a key prefix at a boundary is still
  redacted, for example "sk-1234-add-login-flow".
- Scan counts from an older build are not comparable with counts from
  this change on.

## Evidence
Scan of the live hub on 2026-11-24, counts only, no value read. Of the
4,714 hits of the openai-api-key pattern, 4,243 sat inside another
string: 1,479 in lowercase names such as "task-...", 2,764 in long
mixed-case runs (2,658 events in Codex transcripts, 7 in Claude Code).
A mirror of the new table over the same events drops the six patterns
from 5,223 hits to 868.

